# Test: `ze generate wireguard keypair` is dispatched and takes no arguments
# Exercises: registry local command "generate wireguard keypair" -> RunWgKeypair
#            (internal/plugins/diag/diag.go, registered in register.go)
# VALIDATES: the three-word command resolves through the local-command registry in
#            the real ze binary, its argument guard rejects a stray token, and the
#            refusal names the command and prints the usage.
# PREVENTS:  a command that resolves nowhere, and a guard that lets an argument
#            through to `wg genkey`.
#
# The SUCCESS path -- `wg genkey`, its output piped into `wg pubkey` on stdin, two
# printed keys -- is NOT here. It needs a fake `wg` that a helper script puts on
# PATH, and the parse suite gives that helper no way to call `ze`. runOneCommand
# (internal/test/runner/parsing.go) builds the child environment with childEnv,
# which adds no PATH entry for the ze binary, so the helper gets `ze: not found`.
# A draft that tried it was measured failing on that. The gap is homed at
# spec-fixit-parse-suite-helper-cannot-invoke-ze.
#
# The second limit this comment used to name is FIXED. `parsingRunner.setupWorkDir`
# materializes through `test.Tmpfs.WriteTo(workDir)`, so a `mode=755` fixture now
# reaches disk executable in the parse suite. The orchestrated `.ci` path still
# flattens the block and re-derives the mode from the file extension; that half is
# recorded in plan/journal/helper-bypassed-by-an-open-coded-copy.md.
#
# The success-path coverage lives in TestRunWgKeypair_PipesGenkeyIntoPubkey
# (internal/plugins/diag/diag_test.go), which shims `wg` and asserts `wg pubkey`
# receives exactly what `wg genkey` printed.

stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONF
# Minimal valid config; the command below never reads it.
bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65533
				remote 65533
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# A stray token must be refused BEFORE RunWgKeypair reaches `wg genkey`. That is
# what the stderr text discriminates: drop the guard and the message becomes a wg
# failure here, or on a host that has wg, a printed keypair on stdout.
cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze generate wireguard keypair extra-arg:exit=1
expect=exit:code=1
expect=stderr:contains=generate wireguard keypair: no arguments accepted
expect=stderr:contains=Usage: ze generate wireguard keypair
reject=stdout:contains=private:
